1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Why is it called a sine WAVE? Watch the circle unroll.

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,800
One point, going round a circle. That is the whole ingredient list.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,200
y = sin t. Now draw its height, over time.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,800
The height IS the wave. Nothing else happened.

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,200
y = cos t. A second point, a quarter turn ahead of the first.

6
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,800
Its height is cos t.

7
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,400
cos t = sin(t + 90°). The blue wave is the gold one, moved.

8
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,800
Slide the gold wave a quarter wavelength and it lands exactly on the blue. Same wave — it just started earlier.

9
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,200
y = A sin(f t). Spin faster and the wave tightens.

10
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:20,800
Draw a bigger circle and the wave gets taller.

11
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,800
Now look at the circle edge-on.

12
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,000
The dot is just going up and down — and it is still drawing the same wave.

13
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
A sine wave is a circle, seen from the side.

14
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,800
Pause. Observe. Learn. Follow for the math behind AI — @observer.collapse
